#{[17592186552467 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552467 #edb/ref 1069 "q-151985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552467 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552467 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3},\\, & 1<x<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=2X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552467 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8460a557-83b6-5efd-a8a5-c94e63881b6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552467 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $1<x<2,$ we have $2<y<4.$\n\n![](images/60/60342277a025cb26cebab2ac534fad4aa1b49fc2285571c303c4e829eb464606.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $2<y<4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $2<y<4,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2(\\frac{y}{2})}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{6}, & \\,2<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X,$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4b19843-0372-523c-be3f-e38cf328fccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552486 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that since the values of $X$ are $0<x<1,$ $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](images/5a/5a2a6f4c5e26819af509e9347fb7aff230927bdd9192ad10a48ce9c951c7be80.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡X\\ge -y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge {e}^{-y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}({e}^{-y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{-y})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot {f}_{X}({e}^{-y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}\\, & y>0 \\\\ 0\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ be a continuous random variable with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x>1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $Y=\\frac{1}{X},$ then what is the probability density function of $Y?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3a2a8f5-8f7b-5578-9df3-f4e28dce2d10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552522 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that the possible values of $X$ are $x\\in (1,\\infty ),$ and $Y=\\frac{1}{X}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X$ when $X>1.$\n Moreover, for $x>1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/16/16f3dc78752c8cfcb6b6968e3d57bb3639a4205e2a32dfd569c8c2ca3c29a589.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{1}{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge \\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
